WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.879
private fundraiser at his Maro Lago resort
in Florida.

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:08.889
Donald Trump was actually asked about his
spending and his explosion of the national

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:09.889
debt.

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:13.350
And according to people in the room, he said,
quote, who the hell Al

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.730
cares about the budget?

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.790
We're going to have a country now, I've been
telling you for years, this is bigger than

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.730
Trump, that the debt and deficits are a political
football.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.810
That's all that they are.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.990
Our elected officials don't really care about
debt and deficit as long as we're going into

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.160
debt for the things they want to spend money
on.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.950
And of course, historically, even though Republicans
are the so-called deficit Hawks, Republicans

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:44.050
are the ones who increase the deficit and
thus more quickly increase the national debt

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.050
as well.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:50.720
And in fact, under Barack Obama deficit growth
slowed while under Donald Trump, it has absolutely

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.400
exploded.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.950
And there is Trump at his Mar-a-Lago resort
in Florida telling donors behind closed doors.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.990
Who the hell cares about the budget?

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.830
We're going to have a country.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:10.400
The annual, uh, the Trump annual deficit passed
$1 trillion in 2019 and Donald Trump had promised

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.400
the opposite.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.909
Trump promised that he would get us a surplus
so big that the debt itself would start shrinking

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:20.360
and completely disappear within eight years.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:27.360
Now, only someone who is extremely gullible
or knows nothing at all about the debt and

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.369
deficit would have even for a second considered
that that might've actually been something

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:32.369
that would happen.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.730
Donald Trump also promised that his tax cuts
would so acutely grow the economy, that the

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.900
deficit would shrink as a result of new economic
productivity.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.270
That also has not happened.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.759
Some Republicans may be believed him, others
didn't, but they didn't care because a lot

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:47.640
of them don't actually care.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.869
And per MSNBC, Donald Trump has now added
two point $6 trillion to the national debt

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:54.110
in just three years.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.630
That's more than Barack Obama added during
his entire second term.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:03.930
The truth is that even Republicans know that
the deficit in and of itself doesn't actually

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:04.930
matter.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:09.729
Here's a funny video of a reporter, John Carl
asking at a briefing, Hey, you know, Trump

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.010
said he'd eliminate the debt.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.110
What happened with all of that?

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.110
Check out the answer

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.110
questions.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.110
One.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.790
You mentioned what the president promised
during the campaign during the campaign.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.569
He also promised that he would eliminate the
national debt within eight years.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:28.010
And as you know, I did that at the end of
his first year was that 20 trillion last year

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:31.620
it went to 21 trillion last month, 22 trillion.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.110
So what happened to that promise?

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.520
I mean the president has added historically
large numbers to the national debt.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.610
Instead of keeping a promise to actually pay
it off

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.580
again in the last administration nearly doubled
the national debt.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.569
And when this president ran for office, he
made a commitment to the American people that

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:50.300
he wouldn't attempt to tackle the debt within
eight years.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.110
This president did that the very first year
that he came to office by sending forth a

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.459
budget that balanced within 10 years.

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:02.959
So wait, Trump promised to try to do this
in eight years, proposed a ridiculous budget

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:07.640
that would have done it in 10 and that's considered
an answer to the question.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.420
And what matters are really more detailed
questions that don't get asked questions like

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.270
what are we spending on that is growing the
deficit?

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:22.240
Are we growing the deficit because we are
paying for health care or education or is

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.120
it because we're paying for weapons as an
example?

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:32.019
Are we growing the deficit or um, uh, increasing
our national debt in a way that improves the

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:34.069
wellbeing of the average American?

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:36.800
Or are we just like going to war with the
countries in the middle East?

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.319
What portion of our GDP is the deficit?

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:43.770
Because remember, the rod dollars of deficit
in a vacuum doesn't really tell us anything.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.430
We need to compare it to the size of the economy.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:54.030
What kind of deficit spending has a high multiplier
economically versus a lower economic multiplier?

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.770
We've talked about that term before.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:04:00.960
I won't go into it now, but again, when we
go back to the importance that Republicans

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.470
themselves place on this issue, they expose
themselves time and again and reveal that

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:12.700
they don't actually care about the debt and
the deficit beyond it being a tool that they

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.040
can use to attack Democrats when it's convenient.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.159
This is bigger than Trump.

00:04:17.159 --> 00:04:22.379
It really is in a way, even though Trump said
this behind closed doors and probably didn't

00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:27.130
intend for it to become public, Trump is being
more honest than most of them.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.030
When he says, who cares about the budget?

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:33.840
When we have a country in the sense that that
means debt and deficit don't matter as long

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.639
as we're doing the things that I want to do
and that the people who are going to vote

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:43.129
for me believe that I should be doing or want
me to do any Republican when Republicans are

00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:46.460
in power, does not care about the deficit.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.800
Any Republican when there is a democratic
president believes that the out of control

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.580
spending is the number one problem and that
we can somehow cut our way to prosperity and

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.169
a better middle middle-class and a better
functioning economy.

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:04.129
It's a farce and I appreciate even if it's
behind closed doors, Donald Trump just saying

00:05:04.129 --> 00:05:05.129
who cares about it?

00:05:05.129 --> 00:05:10.599
And then of course, reminder, anytime we talk
about this, we have to continue to drill this

00:05:10.599 --> 00:05:12.249
repeatedly.

00:05:12.249 --> 00:05:19.580
We never hear this question asked by Republicans
except for like the occasional Rand Paul libertarian.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.529
We never hear the question, but how would
we pay for this war?

00:05:24.529 --> 00:05:29.259
Remember when it looked like we might be going
to war with Iran a couple of weeks ago?

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:33.960
I think Rand Paul said something about how
much it would cost, although it might've just

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.339
been about like his non-interventionist stuff
that he was complaining.

00:05:37.339 --> 00:05:42.069
Uh, but there's no questions from Republicans
about can we afford war with Iran?

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:43.069
What would be the cost?

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:44.069
Where are we going to get the money?

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:45.069
What about the deficit?

00:05:45.069 --> 00:05:46.069
What about the debt?

00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:49.370
And yet we hear all the time, we can't afford
health insurance.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.819
We can't afford free public college.

00:05:51.819 --> 00:05:57.219
We can't afford to pay for maternity, maternity
leave or paid sick time or whatever the case

00:05:57.219 --> 00:05:58.219
may be.

00:05:58.219 --> 00:06:02.050
It is merely a political football used when
it is convenient.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.879
Don't for a second think that it is anything
other than that.

